# 4. FARM STATUS AND COLOR LOGIC

The screenshot uses four visual states:

| Status | Color | Display label |
|---|---|---|
| Healthy | Green | Healthy |
| Moderate | Amber | Moderate |
| At risk | Red | At Risk |
| No data | Grey | No Data |

These are presentation states, not necessarily raw data values.

The system must obtain the status from the farm's latest valid assessment.

It must not infer a farm's health merely from the color of its satellite imagery.

## 4.1 Status enum

```ts
type FarmHealthStatus =
  | "healthy"
  | "moderate"
  | "at_risk"
  | "no_data";
```

Color configuration:

```ts
const FARM_STATUS_COLORS = {
  healthy: {
    main: "#35E66B",
    background: "rgba(23, 163, 91, 0.20)",
    text: "#80F3B0"
  },

  moderate: {
    main: "#FFB21A",
    background: "rgba(211, 139, 17, 0.20)",
    text: "#FFD16B"
  },

  at_risk: {
    main: "#FF394C",
    background: "rgba(195, 43, 55, 0.20)",
    text: "#FF8992"
  },

  no_data: {
    main: "#89949D",
    background: "rgba(115, 128, 138, 0.20)",
    text: "#BBC3CA"
  }
};
```

## 4.2 Status calculation

A recommended backend assessment model:

```ts
interface FarmAssessment {
  farmId: string;
  status: FarmHealthStatus;

  score?: number;

  assessedAt: string;
  assessmentVersion: string;

  confidence?: number;

  explanation?: string;
}
```

The status calculation should be performed by the analysis system, not invented by the Maps frontend.

A possible assessment rule:

```ts
function classifyFarmHealth(
  score: number | null
): FarmHealthStatus {

  if (score === null) {
    return "no_data";
  }

  if (score >= 75) {
    return "healthy";
  }

  if (score >= 45) {
    return "moderate";
  }

  return "at_risk";
}
```

The thresholds above are example product rules only. They must be agreed with the agricultural assessment methodology and must not be treated as validated agronomic thresholds.

The Maps API should return the final status and assessment date.

