# 11. FARM SELECTION AND MAP SYNCHRONIZATION

This is one of the most important interaction systems.

The farm sidebar and map must always share the same selection state.

Selecting a farm from the sidebar:

1. Update selected farm ID.
2. Highlight the sidebar card.
3. Highlight the polygon.
4. Calculate the farm's geographic bounds.
5. Fit the map to the selected farm.
6. Display the area label.
7. Keep all other farm boundaries visible where appropriate.

Example:

```ts
const [selectedFarmId, setSelectedFarmId] =
  useState<string | null>(null);

function selectFarm(farmId: string) {

  setSelectedFarmId(farmId);

  const farm = farms.find(
    item => item.id === farmId
  );

  if (!farm) return;

  setSelectedFarmSource(farm);

  const bounds = turf.bbox(farm.geometry);

  map.fitBounds(
    [
      [bounds[0], bounds[1]],
      [bounds[2], bounds[3]]
    ],
    {
      padding: {
        top: 100,
        bottom: 100,
        left: 100,
        right: 100
      },

      maxZoom: 17,

      duration: 1200
    }
  );
}
```

Use a projection-aware bounds implementation where appropriate.

A simple minimum/maximum longitude calculation needs special treatment for geometries crossing the international date line.

## 11.1 Selecting from the map

The reverse interaction must also work.

When the user clicks a farm polygon:

```ts
map.on("click", "farm-fill", event => {

  const feature = event.features?.[0];

  if (!feature) return;

  const farmId = feature.properties.farmId;

  setSelectedFarmId(farmId);

});
```

This should also highlight the corresponding sidebar card.

If the farm is not currently visible because the sidebar is filtered, the application should clear or adjust the filter so the selection remains discoverable.

Do not trigger farm selection when the user is actively drawing or editing geometry.

