# 17. BACKEND API CONTRACTS

The frontend needs a clear API contract.

## 17.1 Fetch all farms

```http
GET /api/farms
```

Response:

```json
{
  "farms": [
    {
      "id": "farm-001",
      "name": "Green Valley Farm",
      "areaHa": 78.3,
      "crop": "Wheat",
      "healthStatus": "healthy",
      "thumbnailUrl": "/media/farms/farm-001.jpg",
      "geometry": {
        "type": "MultiPolygon",
        "coordinates": []
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

The coordinates array above is intentionally abbreviated. The real response must contain valid GeoJSON coordinates.

Only return farms belonging to the authenticated user.

For large collections, use pagination or a compact farm-list endpoint.

## 17.2 Fetch farms within the current map bounds

```http
GET /api/farms/map
  ?west=...
  &south=...
  &east=...
  &north=...
```

Backend query:

```sql
SELECT
  id,
  name,
  geometry,
  health_status,
  area_m2

FROM farms

WHERE user_id = :authenticated_user

AND deleted_at IS NULL

AND ST_Intersects(
  geometry,
  ST_MakeEnvelope(
    :west,
    :south,
    :east,
    :north,
    4326
  )
);
```

Use appropriate geographic handling when the bounds cross the international date line.

For extremely large datasets, consider vector tiles instead of sending all GeoJSON coordinates to the browser.

## 17.3 Fetch one farm

```http
GET /api/farms/:farmId
```

Returns:

- Farm identity.
- Geometry.
- Calculated area.
- Perimeter.
- Centroid.
- Current crop.
- Latest assessment status.
- Last updated timestamp.

## 17.4 Create a farm

```http
POST /api/farms
```

Request:

```json
{
  "name": "Green Valley Farm",
  "crop": "Wheat",
  "geometry": {
    "type": "Polygon",
    "coordinates": []
  }
}
```

The server validates geometry, calculates area and perimeter, checks ownership and persists the record.

## 17.5 Update farm boundary

```http
PATCH /api/farms/:farmId/boundary
```

Request:

```json
{
  "geometry": {
    "type": "Polygon",
    "coordinates": []
  },
  "expectedVersion": 3
}
```

Use optimistic concurrency control.

If another user has changed the geometry since version 3, return a conflict response instead of silently overwriting their work.

## 17.6 Delete or archive a farm

```http
DELETE /api/farms/:farmId
```

Use a soft-delete strategy if the wider application requires auditability and recovery.

Do not physically delete related analysis, evidence or historical data without an explicit retention policy.

